# Pastimes (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, phase 7): what residents do when the work's done. Games, songs, music, dancing,
# tall tales, riddles, wagers, sports, idle crafts, the pups' games and the old's, courting, gossip as a sport; by
# temper and humour, by wealth (the Heights' cards and concerts, the Sump's dice), by season, place and region.
# Alternatives are written in parallel: the second reply answers the second remark. A speaker-tagged reply marks the
# scene and has its own close (a@mark); where a tagged reply asks what the default ones ask, the default closes serve
# both. Facts that only shape one line sit in that line's tag, not in `when`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Games: dice, knucklebones, the board

scene e2_pt_knucklebones
when topic=pastimes
a : Fancy a round of knucklebones? | Where'd I put my knucklebones? | I was the best at knucklebones on our street, once.
b[child] #pup : Yes! I can do onesies AND twosies!
b[old] #old : My paws are too stiff for it now. I'll watch, and tell you what you did wrong.
b[warmth=cold] #cold : It's a pups' game.
b #plain : Go on, then. One round. | Pocket. Other pocket. | Once? What happened?
a@pup : Show me, then. Twosies first.
a@old : You would, too.
a@cold : Then you'll not mind losing at it.
a@plain : Loser buys the cider. | Found them. Your throw. | Grew up. Lost my touch and two of the bones.

scene e2_pt_fox_and_geese
when topic=pastimes
a : You moved that fox backwards last night. Foxes don't go backwards. | Fox-and-geese tonight? I've a new board. | I've never once beaten my mother at fox-and-geese.
b[humour=merry] #merry : I love that game. I always lose and I always laugh.
b[honesty=sly] #sly : I'm very good at fox-and-geese. Ask anyone. Well. Ask me.
b #plain : They do in our house. | Who carved it? | Nobody beats mothers at fox-and-geese.
a@merry : Then play me. I like winning.
a@sly : I'll ask around.
a@plain : Then your house plays it wrong. | A carter, for a sack of oats. | She plays the geese like a general.

scene e2_pt_board_argument
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|family|spouses
a : That last game. You cheated and we both know it. | I've been thinking about that game all day. | Rematch. Tonight. No excuses.
b[honesty=frank] #owned : I did. I'm sorry. I'd do it again.
b[honesty=sly] #sly : Prove it.
b : I won fair. | Still sore? | You'll only lose again.
a@sly : I will, one day, and you'll be sorry.
a@owned : Well. At least you're honest about it.
a : The geese were a square off and you know it. | You took my fox with a goose that wasn't there. | Bring the board. Bring your excuses too.

scene e2_pt_dice_cup
when topic=pastimes
a : The dice hate me. Truly. | Lucky dice, these. Bone, older than my grandmother. | Seven throws without a six. Seven.
b[wealth=poor] #warned : Don't play for #money# you haven't got. I learned that one the hard way.
b[piety=devout] #devout : The Bright One frowns on dice, they say. Not very hard, mind.
b : Dice hate everyone equally. | Lucky for who? | Then the eighth is yours.
a@warned : I know. I know.
a@devout : Not hard enough to stop me.
a : Not equally. Me more. | For whoever's holding them. | That's what I said the last six times.

scene e2_pt_dice_sump
when topic=pastimes a.quarter=sump
a : Dice behind the Ropewalk tonight. You in? | Lost my boots at dice. Won them back. Lost them again. | Old Tanner's using loaded dice. Everyone knows.
b[quarter=sump] #sump : I'm in. I've two pennies and a feeling.
b[quarter=heights] #heights : Dice? In an alley? How very... lively.
b #plain : Not tonight. | Where are they now? | And everyone still plays him?
a@sump : Bring the feeling. Leave the pennies.
a@heights : Livelier than your whist, I'll bet.
a@plain : Your loss. Or your gain. | On a sailor's feet. | Where else would we play?

scene e2_pt_cards_heights
when topic=pastimes a.quarter=heights
a[job!=servant] : The Ashfords play whist till the small hours. Ruinous. | Cards at Lady Merrow's again. I lost a brooch. | One more evening of polite cards and I'll scream.
b[quarter=heights] #heights : And you'll go again next week. We all will.
b[quarter=sump] #sump : Cards all night. Must be nice, having a night to waste.
b #plain : Who won? | Why go? | Then scream. It'd liven them up.
a@heights : We will. Gods help us.
a@sump : It's not as nice as it sounds. Truly.
a@plain : Her, of course. Always her. | One goes. That's the Heights. | They'd only think it was a song.

scene e2_pt_concert_heights
when topic=pastimes a.wealth=rich
a : There's music at Greatly House on Restday. Strings from the south. | The harpist last night was divine. | I fell asleep at the concert. In the front row.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : We've a seat at those. Join us, next time.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #poor : I hear music free, from the street outside.
b #plain : Strings? What, fiddles? | Divine how? | Did anyone see?
a@rich : I might. If I can stay awake.
a@poor : The best seat's often outside, I'm told.
a@plain : Fiddles in better coats. | Like rain, if rain were gold. | The harpist. She glared at me all through the second half.

scene e2_pt_wager_rain
when topic=pastimes
a[stage=adult|old] : Bet you a penny it rains before noon. | I'll wager you can't stand on one leg till the bell. | I bet the baker's late opening tomorrow.
b[wealth=destitute] #broke : I haven't a penny to bet. I'll bet you a turnip.
b[humour=merry] #merry : Done! And double it, for the fun.
b[honesty=guarded] #nobet : I don't bet.
b : Done. | Easy. Watch me. | He's never late.
a@broke : A turnip it is.
a@merry : Double it is. You'll be sorry.
a@nobet : Suit yourself. I'd have won.
a : Shake on it, then. | I'm counting. Loudly. | He was late Restday last. And the one before.

scene e2_pt_wager_lost
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|acquaintances|rivals
a : Lost a penny on the ploughing match. Never again. | Won a whole cheese on a wager. A whole one. | Never bet against a goose. I learned that this week.
b[honesty=sly] #sly : Who were you betting with? I might know them. I might owe them.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : A penny! You'd think it was the Greatly fortune.
b #plain : Until the next one. | What was the wager? | What did the goose do?
a@sly : Then I'll not say.
a@rich : It was to me.
a@plain : Next time I'll bet on the ox. | That I could eat it in a day. I lost that one. | Won. Then bit me.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Songs, music, dancing

scene e2_pt_song_stuck
when topic=pastimes
a : I've had that miller's song in my head since dawn. | Who taught the pups that rhyme? It's everywhere. | Hum something. Anything. Get this tune out of my head.
b[humour=merry] #merry : I know just the cure. A worse song! Oh, she went down to the mill...
b[talk=quiet] #quiet : ... Hm-hm-hmm.
b #plain : Which one? | Some fool at the well. | Hm hm hmm. Better?
a@merry : No! No more songs.
a@quiet : ... Thank you. I think.
a@plain : Don't you start. | I'll find them. | Worse. Now I've got both.

scene e2_pt_singing_badly
when topic=pastimes
a : My neighbour sings when they wash. Every morning. | Was that you singing last night? | I sang at the tavern. They clapped. I think they clapped.
b[humour=dry] #dry : The street's been praying for rain, to drown it.
b[warmth=warm] #warm : Singing's singing. Better than a silent house.
b #plain : Well? Any good? | That was a dying cat. Wasn't it? | Clapped, or clapped you out?
a@dry : The rain's no help. It sings louder.
a@warm : I suppose it is.
a@plain : Like a gull with a cold. | I'm not saying. | Clapped. I'm choosing clapped.

scene e2_pt_fiddle_learning
when topic=pastimes
a : I'm learning the fiddle. | Somebody's been sawing at a fiddle three nights running. | My grandfather's fiddle's sat on the wall for years.
b[old] #old : Took me ten winters to play a tune worth hearing.
b[humour=dry] #dry : Fiddles. The cat's natural enemy.
b #plain : How's it going? | Learner, by the sound. | Take it down. Play it.
a@old : Ten! I'll be grey.
a@dry : The cat agrees.
a@plain : The cat leaves the room. | It was me. Don't. | It's got three strings. It wants four.

scene e2_pt_pipes
when topic=pastimes
a : Hear the pipes down the lane? | I made a pipe from a willow stick. It plays two notes. | There's no sound sadder than pipes at dusk.
b[stage=young] #pup : I want a pipe! Can I have a pipe? I'll only play it a little.
b[humour=merry] #merry : Nothing like pipes. Makes my tail go.
b #plain : Someone's courting, I'll bet. | Which two? | Fiddle at dusk. That's sadder.
a@pup : I'll make you one. Your mother'll never forgive me.
a@merry : I can see it going.
a@plain : Or someone's lost a bet. | High and wrong. | You've a hard heart.

scene e2_pt_dance_paws
when topic=pastimes
a : Are you dancing at the next gathering? | I've two left hind paws. Always have. | I trod on three tails at the last dance.
b[stage=old] #old : I'll dance one. Slowly. Then I'll sit and judge the rest of you.
b[talk=quiet] #quiet : I'll hold the coats.
b #plain : If someone asks. | Everyone does. | Only three?
a@old : Judge kindly.
a@quiet : Every gathering needs a coat-holder.
a@plain : Someone will. | Some more than others. | Three that complained.

scene e2_pt_jig_learning
when topic=pastimes a.stage=young|adolescent
a : Will you teach me the long jig? | Is it true you used to dance at the fair? | My cousin won't dance with me. Says I hop.
b[stage=old] #taught : Stand up. Paw there. No, there. Now the turn.
b : I'm no dancer. | Once, a long time ago. | You do hop.
a@taught : Like this? Like this!
a : Please? Just the first steps. | Show me! Just one turn! | Everyone hops at first!

scene e2_pt_ballad_drowned
when topic=pastimes here.place=tavern|home
a : Sing the one about the drowned sailor. | Who knows the end of the Lighthouse Keeper? | Not that song. Not tonight.
b[humour=merry] #goose : Something cheerful! The goose that married a duke!
b[job=sailor] #sailor : Not the sea songs. Bad luck on land and worse at sea.
b : I only know the chorus. | Nobody. That's why it's sad. | Then which?
a@goose : Not the goose. Anything but the goose.
a@sailor : Sorry. Forgot. Something else, then.
a : Then sing the chorus loud. | Make one up. | Something with a dance in it.

scene e2_pt_round
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|family
a : Start a round. I'll come in after you. | You always come in early in a round. | Remember singing rounds on the cart home?
b[humour=merry] #merry : Round the well and round the well, and who's the one to... Your turn!
b #plain : You start. You're louder. | I come in when I'm ready. | Till the carter told us to hush.
a@merry : ...round the well and round the well! There.
a@plain : Louder isn't better. | Ready's too soon. | We sang it worse after that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Tales, riddles, gossip

scene e2_pt_tall_tale_fish
when topic=pastimes
a : Caught a pike yesterday as long as my arm. | My uncle once fought a bear for a ham. | I knew a wolf who outran a pony.
b[humour=dry] #dry : And it grows every time you tell it.
b[honesty=frank] #doubt : You didn't.
b : Did you eat it? | Did he win? | Over what distance?
a@dry : Only by a paw or so.
a@doubt : Did! Near enough.
a : Every bit, and the head. | He got half. The bear got the other half. | To the next tavern. He was thirsty.

scene e2_pt_tall_tale_contest
when topic=pastimes here.place=tavern
a : Tall tale contest here, last night. You missed it. | Old Brindle won the tall tales again. | I'm entering the tall tales next time. I've a good one.
b : Who won? | What did he tell? | Tell me, then. Practise.
a : A fisher. Said he caught the moon in a net. | That he'd been to the bottom of the Sound and back. | It starts with a goose. You'll see.
b?[humour=merry] : I once caught the sun. Burnt my paws.

scene e2_pt_storytelling_winter
when topic=pastimes here.season=winter
a : Long nights. Good for stories. | Tell us a story, the one with the frost giant. | My grandmother told stories till the fire went out.
b[stage=old] #old : I'll tell you one about a winter worse than this.
b[talk=quiet] #quiet : I'm no teller. I'll listen.
b #plain : They are. | Which one? | Mine fell asleep halfway through hers.
a@old : Every winter's worse than this one, to hear the old tell it. Go on.
a@quiet : Listening's half of it.
a@plain : And this one's the longest yet. | The one where he eats the mountain. | And we'd finish them ourselves.

scene e2_pt_storyteller_visit
when topic=pastimes here.place=tavern|street|market
a : There's a storyteller come to town. | A tale-teller by the well, all hat and voice. | That teller last night had me crying in my cup.
b[stage=young] #pup : Does he do monsters? I like monsters.
b[warmth=cold] #cold : Paid to lie. Good work if you can get it.
b #plain : Is he any good? | What did he tell? | What was it about?
a@pup : Monsters, and worse. Ask your mother first.
a@cold : Better than paid to sulk.
a@plain : Good enough to get my penny. | The drowned bells of Glasswater. | A mother and her lost pup. Don't make me tell it.

scene e2_pt_riddle_mouth
when topic=pastimes
a : Riddle for you. What has a mouth but never eats? | What runs but never walks? | What's yours but everyone else uses it?
b[job=scholar] #knew : That's an old one. You'll have to try harder than that.
b[stage=young] : Ooh! Ooh! Is it... a ghost?
b : A sack? | A pup at bedtime? | My cart?
a@knew : Fine. Clever paws. I'll find a harder one.
a : A river. | A river. Water, anyway. | Your name. Everyone uses it.

scene e2_pt_riddle_trade
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|acquaintances|family
a : Riddle that's beaten the whole street. The more you take, the more you leave behind. What? | Riddle me this. What gets wetter the more it dries? | Here's one. What can you catch but never throw?
b[drive=ambitious] #quick : Easy. Next.
b[job=scholar] #quick : Ha. I had that one off my grandmother.
b : A purse? | A fish? | A ball?
a@quick : Fine, clever paws. I'll find a harder one.
a : Footsteps. Nobody gets it. | A towel. Everyone forgets the towel. | A cold. Nobody's ever thrown one.

scene e2_pt_gossip_sport
when topic=pastimes
a : Bench by the well, an hour, best entertainment in town. | Gossip's free and better than any play. | I've heard three things this morning, and one of them's true.
b[talk=chatty] #chatty : Oh, sit down, sit down. Have you heard about the cooper?
b[talk=quiet] #quiet : I just listen. You'd be amazed what folk say in front of a quiet one.
b #plain : What's the news? | Better than the chaplain's sermons, anyway. | Which one?
a@chatty : No! Tell me. Everything.
a@quiet : I'll remember that. And watch what I say near you.
a@plain : Not here. Later. | And shorter. | I'll tell you when I've worked it out.

scene e2_pt_gossip_well
when topic=pastimes here.place=street|market
a : That's two days running the cooper's wife has gone to the mill alone. | Did you see who walked home with who last night? | Somebody's bought a new coat and won't say how.
b[honesty=frank] #frank : Leave folk be. It's their business.
b[talk=chatty] #chatty : Ooh. Tell me everything.
b #plain : Has she now. | No. Who? | Has he now.
a@frank : I'm only saying.
a@chatty : Not here. Later. Folk listen.
a@plain : I'm only saying. | Not here. Folk listen. | Not saying. Just wondering. Aloud.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Quiet pastimes: walks, carving, needles, books, fishing

scene e2_pt_carving
when topic=pastimes
a : I've been whittling a little fox. | What are you carving there? | Cut my paw carving a spoon. A spoon!
b[job=woodcutter|crafter] #crafter : Mind the grain. The grain decides. You just follow.
b[stage=young] #pup : Can I see? Can you make a wolf? A big one?
b #plain : Show me. | A spoon. Or a ladle. It hasn't decided. | Spoons are dangerous.
a@crafter : The grain and I aren't speaking.
a@pup : A small one. Big ones take wood.
a@plain : It's got three legs so far. | It'll tell me. | Never again. Bowls next.

scene e2_pt_needlework
when topic=pastimes
a : I've been at this sampler since midwinter. | My stitching's gone wonky again. | Embroidered a hen on my apron. Looks like a cloud.
b[stage=old] #old : Small stitches, steady paw. And a good lamp, or you'll go blind as I am.
b[job=sailor] #sailor : Sailors sew better than anyone. Sails, wounds, buttons.
b #plain : Nearly done? | Unpick it. | Clouds are nice.
a@old : A good lamp. I'll find one.
a@sailor : Sew my hen, then.
a@plain : Two letters to go. | Not again. | A cloud with a beak.

scene e2_pt_reading
when topic=pastimes
a : I've been reading of an evening. | Can you read? Honestly? | I borrowed a book from the chapel. Don't tell anyone.
b[job=scholar] #scholar : Books! Good. Whatever it is, skip the third volume. There's always a third volume.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #poor : Never learned. Who had the time?
b[job=priest] #priest : The chapel's books are there to be read. Mind the candle wax.
b #plain : What sort of book? | Bits. My name, and prices. | Why not?
a@scholar : There is. I'm in it.
a@poor : I'll teach you. Your name first.
a@priest : I'll mind it.
a@plain : A long one, about ships. | Same as me, then. | The chaplain's quick to ask what you thought.

scene e2_pt_scholar_reading
when topic=pastimes a.job=scholar
a : I've found a book of old songs, every verse written down. | I sat up reading till the candle drowned. | Someone's torn pages from the chapel's almanac.
b[job=scholar] #scholar : Lend me whatever you're reading. I've read all mine twice.
b[humour=dry] #dry : Exciting life you lead.
b #plain : Can you sing any? | Good book? | Who'd do that?
a@scholar : When I'm done. Which is never.
a@dry : It is. Somebody has to.
a@plain : Not well. Not at all, really. | Bad book. Couldn't stop. | Someone who needed kindling.

scene e2_pt_walk
when topic=pastimes
a : I walked out past the last houses after supper. | A walk does you more good than any healer. | I like walking when everyone's abed.
b[stage=old] #old : A lap round the chapel's my limit now.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : Alone? After dark? I couldn't.
b #plain : See anything? | True. | Every noise a robber, though.
a@old : That's a walk. That counts.
a@nervy : It's quieter than you'd think.
a@plain : A hare and two stars. | The healer said that, too. | Mostly owls.

scene e2_pt_evening_stroll
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|spouses
a[here.time=dusk] : Walk with me a while? | Round the long way home? | Let's go up the hill and watch the light go.
b[tired=yes] #tired : My feet say no. My heart says yes. My feet win.
b #plain : Go on. | Why not. | Last one up buys the cider.
a@tired : Then I'll sit with your feet instead.
a@plain : Slowly, then. | The long way's prettier. | You'll lose. You always stop for breath.

scene e2_pt_fishing_pleasure
when topic=pastimes
a : Took a line down to the water after work. Just to sit. | Nothing beats an afternoon with a rod. | Fished all day. Caught a boot.
b[job=fisher] #fisher : Fishing for fun. I'll never understand it. That's my workday.
b[drive=restless] #restless : All day sitting? I'd go mad.
b #plain : Catch anything? | Nothing. | Whose boot?
a@fisher : That's why it's fun. Nobody's counting.
a@restless : That's the point of it.
a@plain : Didn't want to. | Two things. Fish and sleep. | Mine, from last summer. Good boot, still.

scene e2_pt_pond_fishing_pups
when topic=pastimes a.stage=young
a : Will you take me fishing? | What's the best worm? | I caught a fish! It got away. But I caught it!
b[stage=adult|old] : When the weather turns. | A red one. Fat. | Then it counts.
b : Can I come too? | A fat one. | It only counts if you keep it.
a : Promise? | I'll find a red one! | It counts! It counts!

scene e2_pt_reading_aloud
when topic=pastimes pair.band=family|spouses
a : Read to me tonight? | You do the voices so well. | Where did we get to in that book?
b[job=scholar] #scholar : One chapter. One. I've work to do.
b #plain : Which bit? | Only the funny ones. | The storm. The ship in the storm.
a@scholar : One chapter. And then one more.
a@plain : The bit with the cook. | You do the captain like the old chaplain. | Then the ship sinks, I bet.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Sports: wrestling, races, the ball

scene e2_pt_wrestling
when topic=pastimes
a : Wrestling on the green Restday. Coming? | Who's the best wrestler in town, would you say? | I threw the smith's lad on the green. Once.
b[job=smith] #smith : I'll wrestle anyone. Nobody'll wrestle me.
b[sex=female] #she : I could put you down before you'd tied your belt.
b[stage=old] #old : I was the best in town once. Ask anyone old enough to remember.
b #plain : Who's wrestling? | Depends who's sober. | Once?
a@smith : I wonder why.
a@she : I don't doubt it.
a@old : I'll ask. Quietly.
a@plain : Anyone fool enough. | The smith, sober. Anyone, drunk. | And then he threw me twice.

scene e2_pt_race
when topic=pastimes
a : Race you to the well. | Who won the race at the last fair? | I ran the hill race. Never again.
b[stage=old] #old : My racing days are done. I'll judge.
b[stage=young] #pup : Race! I'm fastest! I'm already going!
b #plain : On three. | A carter's lass. Flew. | Did you finish?
a@old : Judge fair, then.
a@pup : Wait for me!
a@plain : One, two... you went early! | Never seen legs like it. | Last. But I finished.

scene e2_pt_ball_game
when topic=pastimes
a : The ball game's on, our street against the next. | Somebody kicked the ball through the chapel window. | My tail's still sore from the ball game.
b[temper=short] #short : Ball games. Somebody always gets hurt, and it's always me.
b[job=guard] #guard : I'll be breaking that up by dusk, mark me.
b #plain : Who's carrying the ball? | Who? | Who stood on it?
a@short : Then stand further back.
a@guard : You will. You always do.
a@plain : Whoever's daft enough. | Everyone, by now. Everyone's sure it was someone else. | Half their team.

scene e2_pt_ball_game_parish
when topic=pastimes here.region=frosthollow|coldwater|oakhaven|mirebridge|larkfield|southmeadow
a[here.season=spring|summer] : Is the ball game on this year? Town against the farms? | Last year's ball game went on till dark. | The farms are training. I've seen them.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : We'll win. We carry sheep all year. A ball's nothing.
b[job=guard] #guard : Mind the walls and the windows.
b #plain : They say so. | And two days after. | Training? For a ball?
a@farm : We'll see. Bring your sheep.
a@guard : Tell the ball.
a@plain : Good. I'll bring a stick. | My knees remember it. | They mean it this year.

scene e2_pt_skittles
when topic=pastimes here.place=tavern
a : Skittles in the yard after? | Who set the pins up crooked? | I knocked eight down with one throw. Eight!
b[job=innkeeper] #keeper : Not in my yard. The last pin went through my window.
b #plain : If you're paying. | You did, last round. | Out of nine?
a@keeper : That wasn't me. That was a good throw.
a@plain : Loser pays. That's how it works. | I never. | Out of nine. Don't spoil it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The pups and the old

scene e2_pt_pups_tag
when topic=pastimes a.stage=young
a : Play hunter and hare with me? | You're it! | I hid in the woodpile and nobody found me. All day.
b[stage=young] #play : I'm the hunter! I'm always the hunter!
b[stage=old] #old : My hunting days are done, little one. I'll count for you.
b[stage=adult] #later : Later. After the chores.
b : Not now. | Am not! | Nobody looked, maybe.
a@play : No, I'm the hunter! You're the hare!
a@old : Count slow! Really slow!
a@later : After the chores is forever.
a : Please! | Are too! | They did! They didn't find me!

scene e2_pt_pups_hide
when topic=pastimes a.stage=adult|old
a : The pups have been at hide-and-seek in the yard all day. | Found a pup asleep in a barrel. Hiding, they said. | The young ones made up a new game. Rules change every turn.
b[parent=yes] #parent : Mine came home with half the yard in their fur.
b[stage=old] #old : We played the same game. Different barrels.
b #plain : Who's winning? | From whom? | Sounds like the market.
a@parent : That's a good game, then.
a@old : Same pups, near enough.
a@plain : Whoever shouts loudest. | From a cousin. Since noon. | Or the town council.

scene e2_pt_pup_marbles
when topic=pastimes a.stage=young
a : I won six marbles off Pip! | Want to see my blue marble? | Pip cheats at marbles. Flicks with a whole leg.
b : Six! | Is it glass? | Everyone does that.
a : Pip cried. A bit. | Real glass. From the glassworks. | I don't! Not much.

scene e2_pt_old_bench
when topic=pastimes a.stage=old
a : I sit on the bench by the chapel and watch the world. | Draughts with the miller's father, every afternoon. Forty years. | I've taken up nothing. I'm good at it.
b[stage=old] #old : Room on the bench for two?
b[stage=young] #pup : Isn't that boring?
b #plain : Sounds restful. | Who wins? | It suits you.
a@old : Always. Shove up the cushion.
a@pup : You'll see. One day you'll see.
a@plain : It's the best seat in town. | He says him. I say me. | Took me years of practice.

scene e2_pt_old_youth_games
when topic=pastimes a.stage=old
a : When I was a pup we played stones and sticks till dark. | Do you lot still play hunter and hare? | We made our own fun, in my day.
b : What's stones and sticks? | Hunter and hare's for babies. | With what?
a : Stones. And sticks. Mostly sticks. | I'll have you know it's a fine game. | Mud. Mostly mud.

scene e2_pt_old_regret
when topic=pastimes a.stage=old
a[pair.band=friends|family] : I always meant to learn the pipes. Never did. | My dancing days. Gods, what dancing days. | I used to sing in the chapel, you know.
b[warmth=warm] #warm : It's not too late. Come round. I'll find you a pipe.
b #plain : Still could. | Tell me. | Sing something now.
a@warm : These paws? Well. Maybe.
a@plain : These paws? | The harvest of the long summer. I wore out my boots. | Ha! You'd run.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Courting, festivals

scene e2_pt_courting_dance
when topic=pastimes a.stage=adolescent|adult
a : Who are you asking to dance at the festival? | Somebody asked me to walk out. I said maybe. | They danced together three times last night. Three.
b[married=yes] #wed : I've done my asking, thank the gods. I just watch now.
b[talk=quiet] #shy : I... nobody. Maybe. Nobody.
b : Not telling. | Maybe! Who? | Three's an announcement.
a@wed : Smug, that's what you are.
a@shy : Ah. Somebody, then.
a : I'll find out. | Not telling. | The whole street's counting.

scene e2_pt_courting_advice
when topic=pastimes a.stage=adolescent
a : How did you know? That they were the one, I mean. | How do you ask someone to dance? | Is it daft to pick wildflowers for someone?
b[married=yes] #wed : They laughed at the same thing I did. That was all.
b[widowed=yes] #widow : You just know. And you don't stop knowing.
b #plain : You just ask. | You hold out your paw and say nothing clever. | It's daft. Do it anyway.
a@wed : That's all? That's it?
a@widow : ... I'm sorry. I didn't think.
a@plain : That's no help. | Nothing clever. I can do that. | Which flowers?

scene e2_pt_festival_dance_soon
when topic=pastimes world.festival=soon
a : Festival's coming. Have you practised your steps? | They're hanging the lanterns for the dancing. | I need new boots for the festival dance.
b[stage=old] #old : I'll be on a bench with a cup, watching.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #poor : Same boots as last year. They know the steps by now.
b #plain : I'll trip either way. | It's always pretty. | Your boots are #good#.
a@old : Save me a place on the bench.
a@poor : Wise boots.
a@plain : Then we'll trip together. | Prettier this year, I hope. | They're held with string.

scene e2_pt_festival_today
when topic=pastimes here.festival
a : #here.festival#! Are you dancing? | Did you see the fiddler at #here.festival#? Paws like smoke. | They've got races going for #here.festival#.
b[stage=young] #pup : I'm going to win the sack race! I've practised!
b[warmth=cold] #cold : I'll watch. Somebody has to.
b #plain : After a cup. | I danced to him. | Who's in it?
a@pup : Then I'll cheer for you.
a@cold : Watch from the front, then.
a@plain : After two. | Badly, I saw. | Half the town and a goat.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- By character

scene e2_pt_merry_vs_dry
when topic=pastimes a.humour=merry b.humour=dry
cooldown 2d
a : What we need is a song and a jig. | Let's get the whole street dancing! | I've a new joke. It's about a goose.
b : Do we. | Let's not. | Do the goose a favour. Keep it.
a : We do! | Too late, I'm halfway down it. | A goose walks into a tavern...
b : And orders the soup. I know it. | Mind the Watch. | And leaves. Wisely.

scene e2_pt_humourless
when topic=pastimes a.drive=ambitious
a : I don't hold with games. Waste of daylight. | What's the point of a riddle? You just tell them the answer. | Dancing. Jumping about for no reason.
b[humour=merry] #merry : The point is the fun of it!
b[humour=dry] #dry : Strong views, for a quiet afternoon.
b #plain : Some folk like it. | That's the point. | There's a reason. You just haven't found it.
a@merry : Fun doesn't pay the rent.
a@dry : I've strong views every afternoon.
a@plain : Some folk like mud. | Strange point. | I'll keep not looking.

scene e2_pt_chatty_quiet
when topic=pastimes a.talk=chatty b.talk=quiet here.place!=tavern
cooldown 2d
a : What do you do with yourself, of an evening? I sing. Badly. And I talk. Well, you know. | Do you never go to the tavern? It's lively. You'd like it. | You should come to the dancing.
b : Sit. | Not often. | Maybe.
a : Sit! Just sit? | Is that a no? | That's a yes. I heard a yes.
b : Sit. It's nice. | It's a no. | It was a maybe.

scene e2_pt_restless
when topic=pastimes a.drive=restless
a : I can't sit still of an evening. Never could. | I've tried carving, cards, singing. Bored of all of them. | I need something to do. Anything.
b[drive=content] #content : I sit by the fire and I'm happy as a cat.
b[drive=ambitious] #ambitious : Learn a second trade. That's what evenings are for.
b #plain : Walk, then. | What's next? | Help me with my woodpile.
a@content : I'd scratch the cat.
a@ambitious : A second trade. Now there's a thought.
a@plain : Walked everywhere. | Wrestling, maybe. Or the pipes. | Not that anything.

scene e2_pt_content
when topic=pastimes a.drive=content
a : Fire, a cup, a chair. That's all I ask of an evening. | I like my evenings dull. Dull and warm. | Same thing every night. Wouldn't change it.
b[drive=restless] #skin : I'd go out of my skin.
b : Can't argue. | Sounds lovely. | No?
a@skin : Then go out of it. I'll keep my chair.
a : Nobody can. | It is. | Not one bit of it.

scene e2_pt_short_temper_game
when topic=pastimes a.temper=short
a : I've thrown the fox-and-geese board in the fire. | Lost at cards again. Somebody's cheating. | Don't talk to me about skittles.
b[temper=calm] #calm : Breathe. It's only a game.
b #plain : Why? | Who? | What happened at skittles?
a@calm : It's never only a game.
a@plain : It burned well. | Everyone. | Don't talk to me about it.

scene e2_pt_devout_restday
when topic=pastimes a.piety=devout
a[here.day=rest] : Restday's for the chapel and the hearth, not dice. | I spend Restday reading the hymns. | The pups were playing ball in the chapel yard. On Restday.
b[piety=devout] #devout : Rightly said.
b : One game won't hurt. | All of it? | Better than in the chapel.
a@devout : Somebody has to say it.
a : That's how it starts. | Twice, some weeks. | Small mercies.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- By wealth and quarter

scene e2_pt_poor_cheap_fun
when topic=pastimes a.wealth=poor|destitute
a : Best things are free. Songs. Gossip. Watching the rich fall in the mud. | Can't afford the tavern. We sing on the step instead. | A night out costs a week's bread.
b[wealth=rich] #mud : I've never fallen in mud.
b : True enough. | Better singing anyway. | Then stay in. Sing.
a@mud : Not yet. I've hopes.
a : And the rich don't know it. | Echoes better. | That's what I do.

scene e2_pt_rich_bored
when topic=pastimes a.wealth=rich
a : I've had a new spinet sent from the south. Nobody plays it. | Hunting party next week. Do come. | Our musicians cost more than the cook.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] #poor : I'd swap you a week of mine for an hour of that.
b[wealth=rich] #rich : One does what one can.
b #plain : Learn it, then. | What are you hunting? | Do they cook?
a@poor : You might regret it. The hour, I mean.
a@rich : One does.
a@plain : At my age? | Whatever the beaters find. | They'd burn water.

scene e2_pt_sump_music
when topic=pastimes a.quarter=sump
a : There's a fiddle going down the Sump every night now. | Blind Harl plays the whistle for pennies by the drain-mouth. | Sump songs are rude. Best songs there are.
b[quarter=heights] #go : You go down there? For music?
b[quarter=sump] #sump : Best nights in the city, and nobody minds your boots.
b : I've heard it. | Is he any good? | Sing me one.
a@go : The best music in Ridgemere's in its worst streets.
a@sump : Nobody minds anything. That's the beauty of it.
a : Every night till the Watch comes. | The best in the city, they say. | Not with the chaplain about.

scene e2_pt_wharf_racing
when topic=pastimes a.quarter=wharf|docks|harbour_hill
a : Boat race in the harbour on Restday. Wharf against the docks. | Rowed in the harbour race once. Came last, upside down. | Gulls make better rowers than the dock crew.
b[job=sailor] #sailor : The docks have it. Every year. Stronger backs.
b : Who's favoured? | Upside down? | Don't say that on the docks.
a@sailor : Stronger backs, softer heads.
a : The wharf, if they stay sober. | Swam the last half. | I'll whisper it.

scene e2_pt_theatre_ridgemere
when topic=pastimes here.region=ridgemere
a : The players are doing a comedy in the Commons yard. | Saw a play last night. A king, a ghost and a goose. | A play about Ridgemere's founding. Very long.
b[quarter=heights] #heights : We had them at the house. Private, of course.
b[quarter=sump] #sump : Snuck in under the stage. Best seat in the yard.
b #plain : Is it funny? | The goose was best, I'll bet. | How long?
a@heights : Of course you did.
a@sump : I'll join you next time.
a@plain : The goose is. | It was. | Three hours, and that's without the singing.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Seasons, weather, places

scene e2_pt_rain_indoors
when topic=pastimes here.weather=rain|storm
a : #rain.capitalize# all day. Nothing for it but cards. | Too wet for the green. Knucklebones by the fire, then. | Wet day. The pups are climbing the walls.
b[stage=young] #puddle : Can we play in the puddles? Can we?
b : Deal me in. | You'll lose. | Mine too. Send them round.
a@puddle : Go on, then. Boots off first.
a : Penny a hand. | I'll take that bet. | Take them yourself.

scene e2_pt_winter_fire
when topic=pastimes here.season=winter
a[here.time=dusk|night] : Fire's lit. Who's telling the first story? | It's #very# #cold# out there. Mulled cider and a song in here. | Nothing to do of a winter night but sing and sleep.
b[stage=old] #old : Me. I've been saving one all year.
b #plain : You. You always do. | Pour me a cup. | And talk.
a@old : Go on, then. We're listening.
a@plain : Fine. Once upon a frost... | Two cups. | And argue.

scene e2_pt_skating
when topic=pastimes here.season=winter
a[here.region=frosthollow|coldwater] : Ice is thick on the pond. Skating tomorrow? | Fell flat on my tail on the ice. | The pups have made a slide down the lane.
b[stage=old] #old : I'll watch. My skating days ended with a broken hip.
b #plain : Bone skates or plain paws? | Everyone does, once. | Someone'll break a leg.
a@old : Watch from the bank, then. Safe.
a@plain : Bone. I tied them on myself. | Twice. Three times. | Someone already did. Twice.

scene e2_pt_glasswater_skating
when topic=pastimes here.region=coldwater
a[here.season=winter] : Glasswater's frozen to the island. Half the town's out on it. | Races on the ice at first light. | Someone's set up a stall on the ice. Hot cider.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : I don't trust it. I heard it groan last night.
b #plain : Is it safe? | Who's racing? | On the ice? Clever.
a@nervy : It groans every night. It's a lake.
a@plain : Safe as the gods want it. | The fishers. They know the ice. | Clever and cold.

scene e2_pt_frosthollow_sledges
when topic=pastimes here.region=frosthollow
a : Sledges on the mine road after the shift. | The pups have taken the ore trays to sledge on. Again. | Snowball fight by the well. Miners against the Watch.
b[job=miner] #miner : Best part of the day, that. Bar none.
b[job=guard] #guard : And I'll be the one picking pups out of the drifts.
b #plain : Mind the bend. | The foreman'll skin them. | Who won?
a@miner : Bar none.
a@guard : Pick me out too, while you're there.
a@plain : The bend's the best bit. | He went down on one himself. | Miners, by a mile.

scene e2_pt_summer_swim
when topic=pastimes here.season=summer
a : Swim in the river after work? | The pups jumped off the bridge all afternoon. | Lying in the long grass. That's my summer.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : The current's stronger than it looks.
b #plain : I'll race you across. | Into what depth? | Watch for adders.
a@nervy : I'll keep to the shallows.
a@plain : I'll beat you. | Deep enough. They lived. | I'll watch for nothing. Too warm.

scene e2_pt_summer_evening
when topic=pastimes here.season=summer
a[here.time=dusk] : Long evenings. Fiddle on the green after supper. | Too warm to sleep. Let's sit out. | They're playing quoits by the inn.
b : I'll bring the cider. | Till the moths drive us in. | Quoits? Who's winning?
a : And I'll bring the cups. | Till the stars, then. | The innkeeper. Funny, that.

scene e2_pt_spring_green
when topic=pastimes here.season=spring
a : First dance on the green since autumn. | The pups are making daisy chains. | Spring. Everyone's courting again.
b[stage=old] #old : Spring makes fools of us all. Lovely fools.
b #plain : About time. | Bless them. | Everyone always is.
a@old : Lovely, mostly.
a@plain : Rain held off, too. | One for my tail, they said. | In spring it shows.

scene e2_pt_autumn_harvest_games
when topic=pastimes here.season=autumn
a[here.region=larkfield|southmeadow] : Harvest games next week. Sheaf-tossing. | I won the sheaf-toss once. Over the barn. | The last sheaf's for the dance.
b[job=farmer] #farm : I've tossed sheaves all month. I'm ready.
b #plain : Who's favoured? | Over the barn? | And the last cup's for me.
a@farm : That's cheating, that is.
a@plain : Anyone but me. | Nearly over the barn. | We'll see who's standing.

scene e2_pt_tavern_evening
when topic=pastimes here.place=tavern
a : Fiddler's late. We'll have to make our own noise. | Dice or darts? | Somebody's started a song. We're lost now.
b[job=innkeeper] #keeper : Make it something they'll drink to.
b #plain : I'll make it. | Darts. Dice cost too much. | Join in, then.
a@keeper : They'll drink to anything.
a@plain : Loud enough? | Darts it is. Mind your paws. | I don't know the words.

scene e2_pt_market_juggler
when topic=pastimes here.place=market
a : There's a juggler by the fish stalls. Five knives! | A puppet show by the stalls. The pups are mad for it. | Someone's selling song-sheets. A penny each.
b[stage=young] #pup : Can I see? Lift me up!
b[humour=dry] #dry : Everyone's a performer, come Marketday.
b #plain : Five? | Is it the one with the king? | Any good ones?
a@pup : Up you come. Mind my ears.
a@dry : Even you.
a@plain : And an apple. | And the dragon. | One about a goose. Rude.

scene e2_pt_shore_stones
when topic=pastimes here.place=shore
a : Skimmed a stone seven times. Seven. | Pups are building forts on the shore. | Nothing like a walk by the water.
b[stage=young] #pup : Can I come? Can I throw stones?
b : Sure it wasn't six? | The water'll take them. | Unless it's raining.
a@pup : Flat ones only. Mind the gulls.
a : Seven. I counted out loud. | They know. That's the game. | Even then.

scene e2_pt_field_rest
when topic=pastimes here.place=field
a : When the work's done, I'll lie in this grass and not move. | Wrestle for the last apple? | Somebody bring a pipe tomorrow. Music for the work.
b[job=farmer|herder] #farm : Done? It's never done.
b #plain : I'll lie beside you. | It's yours. I'm too tired. | Work songs, then.
a@farm : It's done when I say.
a@plain : Good. | Hah. Easy. | The one about the farmer's boots.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Regions

scene e2_pt_larkfield_shearing_race
when topic=pastimes here.region=larkfield
a : Shearing race on the downs next month. | The Larkfield fiddlers are the best in the realm. | Dancers came over from Southmeadow. Bells on their boots.
b[job=herder] #herder : I'll shear three while they shear two.
b[from=southmeadow] #south : Larkfield thinks a lot of itself. Southmeadow knows better.
b #plain : Who's in it? | Says Larkfield. | They'll be bells on everything soon.
a@herder : Then I know where my penny's going.
a@south : Southmeadow would say that.
a@plain : Every herder on the downs. | Says everyone who's heard them. | Bells on the sheep, next.

scene e2_pt_mirebridge_punts
when topic=pastimes here.region=mirebridge
a : Punt races on the Long River, Restday. | Lost my pole in the punt race. Stood there, drifting. | Who'll win the eel-throwing this year?
b[job=fisher] #fisher : Waste of a good Restday, the lot of it. I'll be there.
b #plain : Who's racing? | Did you get it back? | Whoever's mad enough.
a@fisher : Course you will.
a@plain : The ferry crews, and anyone with a punt. | A heron was standing on it. | Mad's the whole fen.

scene e2_pt_mirebridge_fen_songs
when topic=pastimes here.region=mirebridge
a[here.time=dusk|night] : Hear them singing out on the fen? | Somebody's playing the reed pipe out on the boards. | Sing me the one about the lantern-lights.
b[stage=old] #old : Fen songs. The words are older than anyone knows.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : I don't like songs out in the dark.
b #plain : Somebody's coming home late. | Sounds like a heron. | Not that one. Not after dark.
a@old : Older than the bridge, I'll bet.
a@nervy : They're only fen folk. Coming home.
a@plain : Coming home happy, then. | A heron with a tune. | Fine. The one about the eel.

scene e2_pt_oakhaven_tree
when topic=pastimes here.region=oakhaven
a : The pups climb the big oak like squirrels. | Carving in the Warmwood. Everyone does it here. | I found a dancing ring in the woods. Mushrooms in a circle.
b[job=woodcutter] #cutter : The Warmwood's older than all of us. Mind it.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : The deep wood's not for playing in. Not after dark.
b #plain : One'll fall. | What do you carve? | Did you step in it?
a@cutter : I always do.
a@nervy : It's daylight. Mostly.
a@plain : They never do. | Owls and spoons. | Course not. Mostly not.

scene e2_pt_southmeadow_cider
when topic=pastimes here.region=southmeadow
a : Cider and fiddles in the orchard tonight. | Apple-pressing at the Cider House on Restday. Sticky to the elbows. | The orchard dance went on till the cocks crowed.
b[stage=old] #old : The orchard dances are the best thing about growing old here.
b #plain : I'll be there. | Every year. | It always does.
a@old : Then I'll grow old here too.
a@plain : Bring a cup. | And smelling of it a week. | And every year the same sore head.

scene e2_pt_coldwater_lake
when topic=pastimes here.region=coldwater
a[here.season=summer|spring] : Boats on Glasswater all afternoon. Bliss. | Swam to the island and back. | Night fishing with lanterns on the lake.
b[temper=nervy] #nervy : The water's deep. Deeper than folk think.
b #plain : Whose boat? | All the way? | Catch anything?
a@nervy : That's what boats are for.
a@plain : The ferryman's, borrowed. Mostly. | Most of the way. A boat took me the rest. | The moon, in the water.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- More everyday

scene e2_pt_new_game
when topic=pastimes
a : Somebody's brought a new game from the city. Cards with painted beasts. | Learned a card game from a sailor. Nobody else knows it. | There's a game going round with pebbles and a board of holes.
b[stage=old] #old : New games. In my day there were three games, and that was plenty.
b #plain : How's it played? | Teach me. | Ah. I lost my supper at that.
a@old : Then this makes four.
a@plain : Nobody's sure. That's half the fun. | I'll win, though. | It's a hard game on suppers.

scene e2_pt_sore_loser
when topic=pastimes pair.band=friends|rivals|family
a : You sulked for a day when I beat you. | You'll not win twice. | Admit it. I'm the better player.
b[temper=short] #short : I didn't sulk. I was tired. Sulkily tired.
b #plain : I never sulk. | Once was luck. | Never.
a@short : Sulkily. Exactly.
a@plain : A whole day. | Luck's a skill. | Then play me again.

scene e2_pt_a_hobby
when topic=pastimes pair.band=acquaintances|strangers
a : What do you do when the work's done? | Have you any pastimes yourself? | Do you play anything? Dice, cards?
b[job=scholar] #own : Read. Then read some more.
b[job=smith] #own : Sleep. Then work.
b[stage=young] #own : Everything!
b[wealth=rich] #own : Music. And whatever the household's arranged.
b #plain : Not much. Sit. Talk. | A bit of carving. | Dice, when I've coin.
a@own : Can't beat it.
a@plain : Can't beat it. | Nice, that. | Mind the dice.

scene e2_pt_songs_of_home
when topic=pastimes pair.same_from=yes
a[here.region=ridgemere from!=ridgemere] : Do you remember the songs from home? | Heard a song from home in the tavern. Nearly cried. | Nobody here dances the way we did at home.
b[from=frosthollow] #song : The miners' hymn. I still hum it.
b[from=larkfield] #song : The shearing songs. You never forget those.
b[from=mirebridge] #song : The fen songs. Every one.
b[from=southmeadow] #song : The orchard songs. Nobody here knows a single one.
b[from=oakhaven] #song : The woodcutters' calls. Half song, half shout.
b[from=coldwater] #song : The boat songs. You row in time or you go round in circles.
b #plain : Some. | Which one? | Nobody could.
a@song : We should sing them, the two of us. Show this city.
a@plain : We should sing them, the two of us. | The one about the long road. | We should show them.
